Priority 1: Fix the Integrated Terminal Completely
The integrated terminal is not behaving correctly. Make it a reliable, usable terminal for everyday development on macOS.
1.1 Support Real Shell Commands
Ensure the terminal runs a genuine interactive macOS shell rather than simulating command execution inside the UI.
Commands that must work include:
code
Bash

pwd
ls
ls -la
cd /path/to/directory
java -version
which java
/usr/libexec/java_home -V
echo "Terminal works"
clear
Also support normal shell features such as:
	•	Relative and absolute paths.
	•	Command history.
	•	Environment variables.
	•	Shell initialization.
	•	Interactive command execution.
	•	Standard output and standard error.
	•	Exit codes.
	•	Long-running processes.
	•	Keyboard input while commands are running.
	•	Ctrl+C to interrupt a running process.
	•	Working-directory changes that persist between commands.
	•	Normal shell prompts and command completion when supported by the terminal engine.
Do not implement cd, ls, or java as special hardcoded commands. Execute them through the actual shell and operating-system environment.
Use the user's configured shell, such as zsh or Bash, with a sensible fallback if the preferred shell is unavailable.
1.2 Use a Persistent Interactive Shell
A common terminal bug is starting a new process for every command. This causes commands such as cd to lose their working-directory changes.
Use one persistent shell session for each terminal instance.
For example, executing:
code
Bash

cd /tmp
pwd
must report /tmp on the second command, and subsequent commands must continue running from that directory.
Do not implement command execution as independent calls to Process or exec for every submitted line if doing so prevents shell state from persisting.
If using a native macOS implementation, evaluate a proper pseudo-terminal (PTY) with a persistent shell process. If using an existing terminal emulator, use its supported process and session APIs.
Do not build a terminal by merely displaying command text and captured output.
1.3 Open the Terminal in the Correct File or Project Directory
The terminal must open in the directory relevant to the user's current work.
Required directory-resolution behavior
Use this priority order:
	1	If a project or workspace folder is open, start in the workspace root.
	2	If an active file belongs to an open project, start in the project root when the user is working in project mode.
	3	If the active file is opened independently, start in the directory containing that file.
	4	If no file or workspace is open, use the configured default terminal directory.
	5	If the selected directory no longer exists or is inaccessible, fall back gracefully and display a useful message.
Support paths containing spaces and Unicode characters.
For example, if the active file is:
/Users/example/Projects/Java Demo/src/Main.java
the terminal should start in the appropriate directory according to the rules above. In standalone-file mode, this would be:
/Users/example/Projects/Java Demo/src
The initial prompt should reflect the actual working directory.
Display the initial directory in the terminal header or status area if the existing design supports it.
1.4 Synchronize Terminal Directory with File Navigation
Implement a clear, predictable relationship between the editor and terminal.
	•	Opening a project should establish its root directory.
	•	Opening a standalone file should make its containing directory available to the terminal.
	•	Opening the terminal should resolve the correct directory.
	•	Switching projects must not accidentally reuse a previous project's directory.
	•	Existing terminal sessions must not have their current directory silently overwritten when the user switches files.
	•	Provide an explicit action such as Open Terminal Here or Change Terminal Directory.
	•	Allow the user to open a terminal in a selected folder.
	•	Provide a way to return to the project root.
	•	Support multiple terminal tabs if practical.
When a terminal is already running, distinguish between its current working directory and the editor's currently selected file directory.
Do not silently change an active shell's directory every time the user switches editor tabs.
1.5 Java Environment Support
Java commands must use the environment available to the application.
Support:
code
Bash

java -version
javac -version
which java
echo $JAVA_HOME
/usr/libexec/java_home -V
Requirements:
	•	Preserve the user's appropriate shell environment.
	•	Initialize the shell correctly for interactive use.
	•	Do not assume Java is installed at a fixed location.
	•	Do not hardcode a JDK path.
	•	Handle JAVA_HOME and PATH correctly.
	•	Show the actual error if Java is not installed or is unavailable.
	•	Support Java versions installed through common macOS installation methods, including Homebrew and standard JDK installers, where their environment is configured.
	•	Ensure the terminal uses the correct Apple Silicon architecture when launching native processes.
Do not silently modify the user's shell configuration or install software.
1.6 Terminal UI and Interaction
Ensure the terminal:
	•	Opens and closes reliably.
	•	Can be resized with the main window.
	•	Supports scrolling through output.
	•	Displays long output without clipping.
	•	Handles ANSI colors when supported by the terminal renderer.
	•	Uses a readable monospace font.
	•	Supports light and dark appearances.
	•	Preserves terminal session state while the panel is hidden and shown.
	•	Provides clear startup and process error messages.
	•	Does not freeze the editor while commands run.
	•	Does not lose keystrokes during rapid typing.
	•	Cleans up child processes appropriately when a session is intentionally terminated.
	•	Distinguishes closing the terminal panel from terminating its shell process.
If using a web-based terminal, choose an appropriate terminal emulator such as xterm.js if compatible with the existing architecture, with a reliable PTY bridge on macOS. Do not simply place a text area inside a panel and label it a terminal.
If using a native terminal implementation, use an appropriate terminal renderer and PTY integration.
1.7 Terminal Security
	•	Never execute commands automatically just because a file is opened.
	•	Never execute arbitrary file contents as shell commands.
	•	Do not silently elevate privileges.
	•	Do not automatically run destructive commands.
	•	Respect user confirmation for terminal termination or other destructive operations where appropriate.
	•	Keep the terminal's process lifecycle and filesystem permissions predictable.

Priority 2: Optimize EditorArea Rendering Performance
Refactor EditorArea to reduce unnecessary rendering work during rapid typing.
The objective is to keep the editor responsive without sacrificing syntax highlighting, font selection, selection behavior, search, or correctness.
2.1 Profile Before Refactoring
Inspect the existing rendering architecture.
Determine whether the editor uses:
	•	SwiftUI and NSTextView.
	•	React components and a web-based editor.
	•	A custom text-rendering component.
	•	A combination of native and web components.
Do not apply React memoization APIs to native SwiftUI components or SwiftUI techniques to React components.
Identify which components and calculations are actually causing expensive updates.
2.2 Memoize Line Gutter Rendering
If EditorArea uses React:
	•	Extract the line-number gutter into a dedicated component.
	•	Use React.memo where the component's inputs are stable.
	•	Pass stable props rather than newly allocated objects on every render.
	•	Use useMemo for expensive derived data only where profiling justifies it.
	•	Use useCallback for relevant event handlers when it prevents unnecessary child renders.
	•	Avoid recreating large arrays of line numbers on every keystroke.
	•	Ensure scrolling and visible-line calculations are efficient.
	•	Use stable keys for rendered lines.
	•	Avoid storing redundant copies of line-count state.
The gutter should update when line counts, visible ranges, font metrics, wrapping behavior, or relevant layout settings change—not simply because unrelated application state changes.
If the editor uses a native component, use the equivalent architecture to avoid rebuilding the entire editor or gutter for unrelated state changes.
2.3 Optimize Syntax Highlighting
	•	Identify the current syntax-highlighting engine.
	•	Avoid recalculating the entire document's syntax on every keystroke for large files.
	•	Use incremental tokenization or parsing when supported.
	•	Recalculate only affected regions where practical.
	•	Correctly handle multiline comments, strings, and delimiters that affect subsequent lines.
	•	Prevent stale asynchronous highlighting results from overwriting newer document state.
	•	Preserve the user's selection and cursor.
	•	Avoid resetting the entire text buffer or attributed string unnecessarily.
	•	Keep syntax highlighting independent of font settings and theme state.
	•	Ensure line insertion and deletion do not desynchronize the gutter.
If using React and a dedicated editor engine, use the engine's incremental update APIs.
If using NSTextView, use an appropriate text-storage and attribute-update strategy rather than replacing the entire attributed string after every change.
2.4 Preserve Correctness
Performance improvements must not break:
	•	Text input.
	•	Cursor positioning.
	•	Selection and copy/paste.
	•	Undo and redo.
	•	Search and replace.
	•	Syntax highlighting.
	•	Language switching.
	•	Line numbers.
	•	Word wrapping.
	•	Font changes.
	•	Theme switching.
	•	Multiple documents and tabs.
	•	File saving and encoding.
Do not memoize components with incorrect equality checks that cause stale content, fonts, themes, or line numbers to remain visible.
Do not add arbitrary caching layers without a clear invalidation strategy.
2.5 Performance Verification
Measure or inspect:
	•	Render frequency during rapid typing.
	•	Time spent updating the gutter.
	•	Syntax-highlighting update cost.
	•	UI responsiveness for large files.
	•	Memory use where practical.
	•	Whether font and theme changes update the correct components.
	•	Whether document switching produces stale rendering.
Add targeted regression tests and provide evidence of the improvements where the available environment permits.

